   "output": "-8.0"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Multiplying `x` by itself `n` times costs O(n) — far too slow for exponents in the billions.",
  "Use `xⁿ = (x^(n/2))²`: compute the **half** power once and square it. If `n` is odd, one extra factor `x` is needed: `xⁿ = x · (x^(n//2))²`.",
  "Each call halves the exponent, so only about log₂(n) multiplications are needed. A negative exponent is handled first with `x⁻ⁿ = (1/x)ⁿ`."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def myPow(self, x: float, n: int) -> float:\n        def helper(base, exp):\n            if exp == 0:\n                return 1.0\n            temp = helper(base, exp // 2)\n            if exp % 2 == 0:\n                return temp * temp\n            else:\n                return base * temp * temp\n\n        if n < 0:\n            x = 1 / x\n            n = -n\n\n        return helper(x, n)",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(log n)",
  "timeWhy": "The exponent is halved on every call.",
  "space": "O(log n)",
  "spaceWhy": "The recursion is about log₂(n) calls deep."
 },
